title: Counting to nine
mode: vhdl
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
cycles: 150
random: rst=0.03
---- intro
A digit on a clock counts 0 to 9, then goes back to 0: it never shows 10 to 15. Check the count before adding:

```
if count = 9 then
    count <= (others => '0');
else
    count <= count + 1;
end if;
```

An `unsigned` can be compared with a plain number.
---- task
Count 0, 1 ... 9, then back to 0, one step per rising edge. When `rst` is 1, go to 0. `q` is the count.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity mod10 is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    rst : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end mod10;

architecture rtl of mod10 is
    signal count : unsigned(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity mod10 is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    rst : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end mod10;

architecture rtl of mod10 is
    signal count : unsigned(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if rst = '1' or count = 9 then
                count <= (others => '0');
            else
                count <= count + 1;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
    q <= std_logic_vector(count);
end rtl;
---- hint1
Both `rst` and reaching 9 send the count back to 0.
---- hint2
`if rst = '1' or count = 9 then count <= (others => '0'); else count <= count + 1; end if;`
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        if rst = '1' or count = 9 then
            count <= (others => '0');
        else
            count <= count + 1;
        end if;
    end if;
end process;
q <= std_logic_vector(count);
```
---- done
Chain two of these, the second counting when the first goes from 9 to 0, and you have 00 to 99: the seconds of a stopwatch. With your 7-segment table, it would show on the board.
